# Scheduling App — Monorepo Guide

## Project Overview

A shift scheduling application for small businesses. Two user roles:
- **Employees** — log in, submit vacation/time-off requests, view their schedule
- **Managers** — manage employees, create schedules (using the Python optimizer), view/approve vacation requests

## Repository Layout

```
(monorepo root)
├── frontend/front/        React 18 + Vite + TypeScript SPA          → port 5173
├── backend-csharp/        ASP.NET Core 9 REST API + PostgreSQL       → port 5029
├── backend-python/        Python FastAPI + OR-Tools optimizer        → port 8000
├── packages/
│   └── scheduler-backend-client/   Auto-generated TypeScript axios client (local only, gitignored)
├── scripts/
│   └── check-and-regenerate-client.js   Regenerates the TypeScript client when openapi.json changes
├── auth0/actions/
│   └── save-new-user.js   Auth0 Post User Registration action script
├── docker-compose.yml     Runs all 4 services (postgres, backend-csharp, backend-python, frontend)
├── .vscode/tasks.json     Cursor/VS Code run tasks
└── .env.example           All environment variables documented
```

## Service Details

### frontend/ — React SPA (port 5173)
- **Tech**: React 18, Vite, TypeScript, TailwindCSS, Auth0, TanStack Query
- **Entry point**: `frontend/front/src/main.tsx`
- **Routes**:
  - `/` — Employee home: submit vacation requests, view upcoming vacations
  - `/info` — Info/help page
  - `/admin-panel/Overview` — Manager dashboard
  - `/admin-panel/Employees` — Manage employees
  - `/admin-panel/Scheduling` — Create schedules (triggers Python optimizer via C# API)
  - `/admin-panel/Vacations` — View and manage all vacation requests
- **API client**: `@noadudai/scheduler-backend-client` — auto-generated locally from the C# API's openapi.json, lives in `packages/scheduler-backend-client/` and is resolved via npm workspaces (no registry, no GitHub PAT needed)
- **Auth**: Auth0 with JWT Bearer tokens

### backend-csharp/ — ASP.NET Core API (port 5029)
- **Tech**: .NET 9, Entity Framework Core, PostgreSQL (Npgsql), Auth0 JWT, Swashbuckle
- **Entry point**: `backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService/Program.cs`
- **Solution file**: `backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService.sln`
- **Swagger UI**: `http://localhost:5029/swagger`
- **Endpoints**:
  - `POST /auth0-maintenance/save-new-user` — HMAC-signed; called by Auth0 Action to persist newly registered users
  - `POST /manager-schedule-actions/create-schedule` — create a shift schedule (JWT required)
  - `GET /manager-schedule-actions/schedules` — list schedules (JWT required)
  - `POST /manager-schedule-actions/change-schedule-status` — update schedule status (JWT required)
  - `POST /user-schedule-preferences-request/date-range-preference-request` — submit vacation request (JWT required)
  - `POST /user-schedule-preferences-request/vacations-by-date-range` — query vacations by date range (JWT required)
- **HMAC verification**: The `RequireHmacSignatureFilter` reads `Auth0:HMAC_SECRET` from config and verifies the `X-Signature` header on `/auth0-maintenance/` endpoints

### backend-python/ — FastAPI Schedule Optimizer (port 8000)
- **Tech**: Python, FastAPI, Google OR-Tools
- **Entry point**: `backend-python/src/server/app.py`
- **Swagger/docs**: `http://localhost:8000/docs`
- **Endpoints**:
  - `GET /create_and_get_schedule_options` — runs OR-Tools constraint solver, returns up to 100 valid schedule options with employee and shift metadata
- **OpenAPI generation**: `python backend-python/generate_openapi.py` dumps the schema to stdout

## How to Run

### Option 1 — Docker Compose (all services)
```bash
cp .env.example .env   # fill in Auth0 values and HMAC_SECRET
docker compose up --build
```

### Option 2 — Cursor Tasks (local dev, recommended)
Open Command Palette (`Cmd+Shift+P`) → `Tasks: Run Task`:
- **Start all (local dev)** — starts postgres in Docker + all 3 services locally in parallel
- **Start C# API (local)** — `dotnet run` in `backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService`
- **Start Python API (local)** — `uvicorn src.server.app:app --reload --port 8000` in `backend-python`
- **Start frontend (local)** — `npm run dev` in `frontend/front` (auto-regenerates API client if needed)
- **Start postgres only** — `docker compose up postgres`
- **Run DB migrations** — `dotnet ef database update`
- **Force regenerate API client** — regenerates TypeScript client from current openapi.json

### First-time setup
```bash
# 1. Set up environment variables
cp .env.example .env   # then fill in Auth0 values

# 2. Start postgres
docker compose up postgres -d

# 3. Run DB migrations
cd backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService && dotnet ef database update && cd ../..

# 4. Set C# secrets (local dev only — do NOT put secrets in appsettings.json)
cd backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService
dotnet user-secrets set "Auth0:Domain" "https://<your-tenant>.auth0.com/"
dotnet user-secrets set "Auth0:Audience" "https://UsersShiftsApi/"
dotnet user-secrets set "Auth0:HMAC_SECRET" "<your-hmac-secret>"
cd ../..

# 5. Set up Python virtualenv
cd backend-python
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cd ..

# 6. Copy frontend env vars
cp frontend/.env.example frontend/front/.env   # fill in Auth0 values
```

## npm Client Auto-Regeneration

The frontend consumes `@noadudai/scheduler-backend-client` which is generated locally from the C# API's `openapi.json`. It lives in `packages/scheduler-backend-client/` and is resolved via npm workspaces — no registry or GitHub PAT needed.

**Requirements:** Docker must be running (used to run `openapitools/openapi-generator-cli` — no Java install needed).

When you run `npm run dev` in `frontend/front/`, the `predev` script (`scripts/check-and-regenerate-client.js`) runs automatically:
1. Hashes `backend-csharp/UserShiftsApiService/openapi.json`
2. Compares to stored hash in `packages/scheduler-backend-client/.openapi-hash`
3. If unchanged and `dist/` exists → skips (~50ms)
4. If changed or missing → builds the C# project, runs openapi-generator-cli via Docker, rebuilds the package (~1-2 min first time, faster after Docker image is cached)

To force regenerate: `Cmd+Shift+P → Tasks: Run Task → Force regenerate API client`

## Environment Variables

| Variable | Service | Description |
|---|---|---|
| `VITE_AUTH0DOMAIN` | frontend | Auth0 tenant domain (e.g. `https://dev-xxx.us.auth0.com/`) |
| `VITE_AUTH0CLIENTID` | frontend | Auth0 SPA application Client ID |
| `VITE_AUTH0AUDIENCE` | frontend | Auth0 API audience identifier |
| `VITE_HOMEPAGEURL` | frontend | Redirect URI after Auth0 login |
| `VITE_BACKEND_BASE_URL` | frontend | C# API base URL (e.g. `http://localhost:5029`) |
| `Auth0__Domain` | backend-csharp | Same value as `VITE_AUTH0DOMAIN` |
| `Auth0__Audience` | backend-csharp | Same value as `VITE_AUTH0AUDIENCE` |
| `Auth0__HMAC_SECRET` | backend-csharp | Shared secret for verifying Auth0 Action HMAC signatures |
| `ConnectionStrings__DefaultConnection` | backend-csharp | PostgreSQL connection string |
| `FrontUrl` | backend-csharp | Frontend origin for CORS (`http://localhost:5173` locally) |
| `POSTGRES_USER` | postgres | DB username (default: `postgres`) |
| `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` | postgres | DB password (default: `postgres`) |
| `POSTGRES_DB` | postgres | DB name (default: `shiftsusers`) |

For local dev: use `dotnet user-secrets` for C# secrets; put frontend vars in `frontend/front/.env`.
For Docker: all vars are loaded from the root `.env` file via `docker-compose.yml`.

## Inter-Service Communication

```
User Browser
  └─► frontend (5173)
        └─► backend-csharp (5029)   [REST, JWT Bearer auth]
              ├─► backend-python (8000)  [HTTP GET /create_and_get_schedule_options]
              └─► PostgreSQL (5432)      [EF Core / Npgsql]

Auth0
  └─► ngrok tunnel
        └─► backend-csharp (5029)   [POST /auth0-maintenance/save-new-user, HMAC-SHA256 signed]
```

## Auth0 Setup

1. Create an Auth0 tenant at https://auth0.com
2. **SPA Application**: create a Single Page Application → copy `Domain` and `Client ID` into `frontend/front/.env`
3. **API**: create an API with identifier `https://UsersShiftsApi/` → copy identifier into both `frontend/front/.env` (`VITE_AUTH0AUDIENCE`) and the C# API config (`Auth0__Audience`)
4. **Post User Registration Action**:
   - Auth0 dashboard → Actions → Flows → Post User Registration → Add Action → Build from scratch
   - Paste the script from `auth0/actions/save-new-user.js`
   - Add two secrets:
     - `HMAC_SECRET` — must exactly match `Auth0__HMAC_SECRET` in the C# API
     - `API_URL` — your ngrok URL for local dev (see below), or production URL
   - Deploy the action

## ngrok Setup (local dev only)

Auth0 Actions run in the cloud and cannot reach `localhost`. ngrok creates a public HTTPS tunnel to your local C# API so Auth0 can call it on new user registration.

```bash
ngrok http 5029
# Example output: Forwarding  https://abc123.ngrok-free.app -> http://localhost:5029
```

After starting ngrok:
1. Copy the HTTPS forwarding URL (e.g. `https://abc123.ngrok-free.app`)
2. Auth0 dashboard → Actions → the Post User Registration action → Secrets tab
3. Update `API_URL` to the new ngrok URL → Save
4. No code change or action redeploy needed — the script reads `API_URL` at runtime

> The ngrok URL changes on every restart. Repeat steps 1–3 each time you restart ngrok.
> In production, set `API_URL` to your stable deployed URL once — no more ngrok needed.
